    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a residential deck project in Vancouver. It's gonna be a cedar deck attached to the main house. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load for the calculations? Also, what are the general principles for figuring out post spacing and beam sizing so we stay within code? I need to make sure the structural integrity is solid.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vancouver's got a great climate for a cedar deck, doesn't it? The key to a sturdy deck is understanding the loads that'll be bearing down on it. Think of it like a big ol' pile of goblins, each one representing a different type of load. The dead load is like the goblin's weight, the weight of the deck itself, including the boards, joists, and any other structural elements. This load is always present, even when no one's standing on the deck.\n\nOn the other hand, the live load is like the goblin's mischief – it's the weight of people, furniture, and other objects that'll be on the deck. This load can vary greatly depending on how the deck's used. For example, if you've got a big party and everyone's dancing on the deck, the live load will be much higher than if it's just a quiet evening at home.\n\nNow, when it comes to calculating these loads, you'll need to consider the International Residential Code (IRC) and the Canadian National Building Code (CNBC). The IRC and CNBC provide tables and formulas for determining the dead load and live load, which you can use to size your deck's structural elements.\n\nFor post",
